# 13 Compile options with no in-pattern spelling: PCRE2_LITERAL,
#    PCRE2_MATCH_UNSET_BACKREF, PCRE2_FIRSTLINE and more
# https://www.pcre.org/current/doc/html/pcre2api.html
#
# Not exposed by PHP. Unlike most compile options these have neither a PHP
# modifier letter nor a (*DIRECTIVE) form, so nothing in a preg pattern
# string can ever reach them.
#
# What it is / what it enables:
#   LITERAL makes the whole pattern plain text with no metacharacters.
#   What preg_quote() does, as an engine option (Java ships this as
#   Pattern.LITERAL).
#   MATCH_UNSET_BACKREF makes a backreference to an unset group match
#   empty instead of failing. That is JavaScript's rule, so JS-dialect
#   patterns behave identically.
#   FIRSTLINE forces any match to start on the first line of the subject.
#   No anchor can express that constraint.
#   MATCH_INVALID_UTF matches UTF patterns over subjects containing
#   invalid UTF-8 instead of failing the whole call.
#   ALLOW_EMPTY_CLASS makes [] a valid class that never matches (the
#   JavaScript rule again) instead of a compile error.
# (Also API-only and not demoed here: ALT_CIRCUMFLEX, ALT_VERBNAMES,
# NEVER_UTF, NEVER_UCP, NEVER_BACKSLASH_C, NO_UTF_CHECK.)

# Expect: 0: a.b
# LITERAL: the dot is just a dot.
/a.b/literal
    a.b

# Expect: No match
# ...so "axb" no longer matches the "any char" reading.
/a.b/literal
    axb

# Expect: 0: b
# MATCH_UNSET_BACKREF: (a)? did not participate and \1 matches empty (the
# JavaScript rule).
/(a)?\1b/match_unset_backref
    b

# Expect: No match
# Default PCRE2 (and therefore PHP) semantics: an unset backreference
# fails.
/(a)?\1b/
    b

# Expect: No match
# FIRSTLINE: the only 'b' is on the second line, so it is out of reach.
/b/firstline
    a\nxb

# Expect: 0: b
# On the first line it matches as usual.
/b/firstline
    b here

# Expect: Failed: error -23: UTF-8 error: illegal byte (0xfe or 0xff) at offset 3
# Baseline: in UTF mode an invalid byte in the subject fails the whole
# call. This is PHP's PREG_BAD_UTF8_ERROR.
/ok/utf
    ok \xff broken

# Expect: 0: ok
# MATCH_INVALID_UTF: the same call matches across the damage.
/ok/utf,match_invalid_utf
    ok \xff broken

# Expect: Failed: error 106 at offset 4: missing terminating ] for character class
#         (followed by pcre2test's caret line)
# Baseline: [] is a compile error in PCRE2 and PHP ("[" then "]y" begins a
# class that never terminates).
/x[]y/

# Expect: No match
# ALLOW_EMPTY_CLASS: [] compiles as a class matching nothing, which is
# JavaScript's rule. The pattern is valid but can never match.
/x[]y/allow_empty_class
    xy
